      "raw_payload": {
        "id": "R1",
        "kind": "hypothesis",
        "claim": "Speculative capability: a deliberately clearance-rich joint could restore the pointer more repeatably than a visually tight joint while accommodating the 18 mm replacement. Repeatability would come from seating order, rather than matching rod width.",
        "delta": "Change the dependency from opposed tight-fit contacts to the same two adjacent plywood datum faces and an insertion stop for each rod. Change the action to end-stop contact, corner seating, then hand tightening. The carrier is a shared plywood cradle with bolt-operated restraint; elastic loops supply seating bias and cord retains releasable hardware. Cut and drill only plywood. Keep both rod pockets and all retained hardware inside the 90 mm cube.",
        "prediction": "Nearest rival: tighter symmetric restraint improves both stiffness and return. The seating-order hypothesis predicts the datum configuration has smaller post-exchange displacement despite visible clearance, including after substitution; the rival predicts tight-fit superiority. If return errors improve but loaded drift increases, repeatability and restraint remain separate unresolved dependencies. The two supplied trials suggest a discriminator, not proof.",
        "probe": "Use the six plywood pieces, eight bolts and nuts, four washers, loops, cord and permitted hand tools to fabricate one captive cradle within 100 minutes. Compare two configurations: datum seating before tightening versus opposed tightening using the same hardware. Before timed exchanges, verify envelope and that loosened hardware stays attached. Within 20 testing minutes, allocate twelve exchanges and four 10 N, ten-second load trials per configuration: five exchanges per original rod followed by two 18 mm substitutions. Record exchange time, target-card return, loaded displacement and retention. Counterbalance configuration order. Stop if retention fails; a stopped run establishes no performance success.",
        "fit": "uncertain",
        "assumptions": [
          "The required rod orientation permits both pockets within the envelope; orientation is unspecified.",
          "Available plywood and bolts can form sufficiently rigid datums, and cord can securely retain hardware without obstructing removal; verify before exchanges.",
          "Defeat conditions include worse return than the rival, target escape under load, any exchange reaching forty seconds, loose components, or exhausted fabrication/testing time. Insufficient measurement resolution or incomplete trials leave the outcome unresolved.",
          "No drilling, adhesive or permanent rod marks are permitted; preload must avoid permanent indentation. This is an enabling experiment, not deployment."
        ],
